<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxiad dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 189 BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Artashesian dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Artaxiad dynasty ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from about 189 BCE to the early 1st century CE. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a>, a former Seleucid governor, declared independence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>'s defeat at Magnesia and built a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> the dynasty briefly ruled an empire from the Caspian to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> reduced Armenia to a client kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Artaxiad dynasty, also called Artashesian, ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> overthrew it. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a>, claimed kinship with the Orontids, the previous ruling dynasty. According to the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Artaxias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zariadres">Zariadres</a> were Seleucid generals given Greater Armenia and Sophene by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>. After the Seleucid defeat at Magnesia in 190 BCE they made themselves autonomous kings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> recognised them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE.</p><p>Scholars now think the two were local figures related to the Orontids. Their Irano-Armenian names and Artaxias's boundary stones, in which he calls himself an Orontid, point that way. Matthew P. Canepa regards the Artaxiads as Iranian dynasts. The later Armenian historian Movses Khorenatsi tells of a conflict in which Artaxias killed King Orontes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>, who reigned 95 to 55 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was briefly the most powerful state east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. He brought the largely autonomous nakharars under central control, took large territories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a> as a new capital. In 83 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> offered him the Seleucid crown, and his realm then reached as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. It stretched, for a short time, from the Caspian to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p>Tigranes had allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. In 69 BCE a Roman army under Lucullus routed him outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, and in 66 BCE Pompey forced his surrender, reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to its former borders and let him keep the throne as an ally. His heir Artavasdes II was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> in 35 BCE and later executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After Artaxias II was murdered, civil war between pro-Roman and pro-Parthian parties followed, and Armenia became a Roman protectorate under Augustus. The dynasty ended in chaos around 12 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Hellenistic influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> peaked under the Artaxiads. Their coins followed Greek models with Greek inscriptions, and some call the kings Philhellenes. Cleopatra, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> such as the historian Metrodorus of Scepsis to court, and Artavasdes II composed Greek tragedies himself. Their religion remained, in the words of James R. Russell, staunchly Zoroastrian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> emerged only later as their undisputed successors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artaxiad dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Artaxiad</b> line ended, Armenia passed through years of rival kings installed by Rome and Parthia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
